    { q: "Why are systematic uncertainties considered harder than statistical ones?", a: "Statistical uncertainty can be computed from the data itself and shrinks as one over the square root of the number of events. A systematic is a bias affecting every measurement identically, so repeating the experiment reproduces the same wrong answer with a small statistical error around it. It must be estimated by deliberately varying assumptions, which means it is only as good as the experimenter's imagination about what could be wrong. Errors nobody thought of contribute nothing to the quoted uncertainty and all of the damage." },
    { q: "Explain what a five-sigma result does and does not tell you.", a: "It says that if only known background processes were present, random fluctuation would produce a result at least this extreme roughly three times in ten million. It is a p-value computed under the background-only hypothesis. It does not give the probability that the discovery is genuine, because that additionally requires a prior probability for the new effect. The five-sigma convention exists because particle physics repeatedly saw three-sigma results disappear with more data." },
    { q: "What is the look-elsewhere effect, and how is it reported?", a: "If you search many independent regions, for example many mass windows in a spectrum, the chance of a large fluctuation appearing somewhere is far higher than the chance of it appearing at any one specified place. Experiments therefore quote local significance, computed at the position of the excess, and global significance, which accounts for the whole searched range via a trials factor. Global significance is always smaller, sometimes turning a four-sigma local excess into under two sigma." },
    { q: "Distinguish standard deviation from standard error, and say why the difference matters.", a: "Standard deviation measures the spread of individual measurements and characterises the noise of the apparatus; it does not decrease as you collect more data. Standard error on the mean is the standard deviation divided by the square root of the number of measurements and describes how well the average is determined, so it does decrease. Confusing them either wildly inflates a quoted uncertainty or makes an apparatus look far more precise than it is." },
    { q: "How does blind analysis protect against experimenter bias, and how does physics differ from fields hit by the replication crisis?", a: "In a blind analysis the selection cuts, background model, fitting method and systematic estimates are all fixed while the answer is concealed, often by masking the signal region or adding a secret offset, and the result is published whatever it turns out to be. This prevents cuts being tuned until the answer looks right. Physics also uses a five-sigma rather than a two-sigma threshold, builds independent experiments to cross-check one another, publishes null results as exclusion limits, and separates statistical from systematic uncertainties so readers can judge each." }
